The plan restores 3 VM(s). A run of it:
  reads 60 GiB of backup disks, each in full
  takes about 23 min (reading at 100 MB/s, a rate measured on older servers, plus 4 minutes to prepare each VM; a faster node takes less)
  reserves 6.8 GiB of the node's RAM while it runs (each VM's memory plus 256 MiB)
Wrote <dir>/plan.yaml: 3 VM(s) from node pve1's backups.
  - Linux VMs checked on SSH (port 22) until you change it to the service each one provides: 100 (web-rocky), 101 (web-debian).
  - Windows VMs checked through their guest agent (the Event Log service): 102 (dc-01). Add the service each one provides.

Next:
  1. Change each probe marked CHANGE to the service that VM provides. The plan is valid as it stands (compliance.frameworks is [NONE]: replace it only to name a standard).
  2. certistack plan <dir>/plan.yaml   (shows what a run would do; changes nothing)
  3. certistack run <dir>/plan.yaml
  Or run init again with --inspect: it looks inside each Linux VM's newest backup, read-only, and probes the services it finds instead of SSH.
